{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "id": "msg_011Cfba443zUimGcWLdrdoKV", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Represent amounts as **integer cents** using the existing `Money` type in `src/billing/money.py`. Never use a float.\n\n```python\nfrom billing.money import Money\n\nprice = Money(cents=1999)            # $19.99, currency defaults to \"USD\"\ntotal = price + Money(cents=500)     # Money(cents=2499, currency=\"USD\")\n```\n\nThe project follows these rules:\n\n- **Use `int` cents everywhere**, in function arguments, dataclass fields and JSON payloads. For example, `Invoice.subtotal_cents: int`, `refund(invoice, amount_cents: int)` and `{\"total_cents\": ...}` in `src/api/checkout.py`.\n- **End field and parameter names in `_cents`**: `unit_cents`, `tax_cents`, `refunded_cents`, and so on.\n- **Store amounts as `BIGINT` columns named `*_cents`** in the database (see `migrations/0001`\u2013`0005`).\n- **Don't add amounts in different currencies.** `Money.__add__` asserts that both currencies match. If you're dealing with more than one currency, carry the currency with the amount by passing `Money` rather than a bare `int`.\n- **Round tax with `decimal.Decimal` and `ROUND_HALF_EVEN`** (banker's rounding) when a calculation produces fractions of a cent, then convert back to whole cents. Don't use `ROUND_HALF_UP`. The team agreed this with finance in March 2026. `tax_for` in `src/billing/tax.py` hasn't been written yet, so you may be the one implementing it.\n\nThese rules come from the project's EngramDB memories (*\"Money is integer cents, never float\"* and *\"Tax uses ROUND_HALF_EVEN\"*), and the code matches them.\n\nTwo other things I noticed:\n- `src/billing/invoices.py:22` sends the total to the ledger service under the key `\"amount\"` rather than `\"amount_cents\"`. That's probably fixed by the external API, but don't copy it as the naming pattern for new code.\n- An EngramDB `query` for \"currency amount money\" returned nothing, even though both memories exist in `.engramdb/memories/`. I found them by searching the files directly. You may want to run `engramdb reindex` or `doctor`."}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 367, "cache_read_input_tokens": 23999, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 367}, "output_tokens": 3, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "not_available"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "ed408f16-2eb7-4ae3-8a57-876a55fd6b34", "uuid": "61ca83e3-01cb-47ed-beb0-8feb1d1a8b4a", "timestamp": "2026-10-01T13:09:11.376Z", "request_id": "req_011Cfba43gACykszhc5DFbFz"}
